    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://niagarajazzfestival.com/events/live-learn-jazz-bebop-in-the-brewery/"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/performer": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": ""
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "📅 DATE: Thursday, April 6, 2017  🕑 TIME: 7:00 PM  🏟️ VENUE: The Exchange Brewery (7 Queen St, Niagara-on-the-Lake, ON)  💵 PRICE:  $25 + HST (Early Bird: $20 + HST); Season’s Pass (Includes Festival Weekend events): $349 + HST     To donate and help support the festival:    * Please note that donations accepted to help the TD Niagara Jazz Festival (Registered Charity: #80107-7033-RR0001) continue to bring live music to Niagara and beyond. Thank you for supporting the arts!        ABOUT THE SHOW  The TD Niagara Jazz Festival presents: LIVE LEARN JAZZ: A Canadiana Suite Part One: BEBOP IN THE BREWERY Featuring KEVIN TURCOTTE     A Canada 150 Celebration    Jazz master Louis Armstrong laid down the foundations for jazz, as we know it. Then came along Dizzy Gillespie &amp; Charlie Parker giving birth to ‘Bebop’ developing what Armstrong had built and taking jazz virtuosity to a whole other level.    Join us for an intimate encounter with renowned Canadian Jazz trumpeter Kevin Turcotte as he explores the fundamentals of Bebop.    Brewed Hops &amp; Bebop.  A match made in heaven!     LIVE – the experience  LEARN – the history  JAZZ – the music    The TD Niagara Jazz Festival is a new and exciting jazz festival dedicated to celebrating the art form of jazz in the Niagara Region. The festival inspires, educates and develops future jazz audiences for generations to come while combining live jazz with a Niagara experience – food, wine, culture and natural wonders.    ALL TD Niagara Jazz Festival events are family friendly – appropriate for jazz lovers of all ages!  ***All artists and venues subject to change. *** * Supported in part by the Ontario 150 Fund (Province of Ontario) and the City of St. Catharines. *"
      }
    ],
